@page "/architecture"

<PageTitle>Hotel Architecture</PageTitle>

<section class="architecture-page" aria-labelledby="architecture-title">
    <header class="architecture-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">A simple map of the Hotel application</p>
        <h1 id="architecture-title">How Hotel works</h1>
        <p>
            Follow a guest booking, see where information is stored, and discover how a change
            reaches the running demo. This is a system overview, not a live traffic monitor.
        </p>
        <p class="diagram-key">
            <span class="key-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            Read each numbered flow from left to right, or top to bottom on a small screen.
            Moving dots are decorative; the numbered steps and labels explain the flow without them.
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="architecture-section" aria-labelledby="runtime-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">What happens while the app is running</p>
                <h2 id="runtime-title">Guest booking</h2>
            </div>
        </div>
        <ol class="flow-track" aria-label="Guest booking request flow">
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">1</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Guest's browser</h3>
                    <p>Browses hotels, checks dates and guests, and confirms a room.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">2</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Hotel web app</h3>
                    <p>Blazor WebAssembly files are served by Azure Static Web Apps.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">3</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Booking API</h3>
                    <p>ASP.NET Core checks availability and applies booking rules.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">4</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Azure SQL</h3>
                    <p>Stores the hotel catalog and confirmed reservations.</p>
                </div>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="flow-note">
            The API accesses SQL with its managed identity and a restricted database role.
            The browser does not connect directly to the database.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="architecture-section" aria-labelledby="routing-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">A separate API capability</p>
                <h2 id="routing-title">Safe runtime routing</h2>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
            When a routing request is made, the API checks policy and Prompt Shields, then looks
            for evidence from the requested knowledge revision. Azure OpenAI is considered only
            when more than one safe worker remains. A failed safety, evidence, or model check
            returns the request for human review.
        </p>
        <ol class="flow-track" aria-label="Runtime routing flow">
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">1</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Booking API</h3>
                    <p>Deterministic C# policy checks the request and eligible workers.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">2</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Content Safety</h3>
                    <p>Prompt Shields checks routing metadata before a suggestion is considered.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">3</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Azure AI Search</h3>
                    <p>Checks for evidence matching the exact requested knowledge revision.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">4</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Optional model suggestion</h3>
                    <p>Agent Framework may ask Azure OpenAI to choose among multiple safe options.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">5</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Bounded decision</h3>
                    <p>The API validates the result; unsafe or failed checks go to human review.</p>
                </div>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="flow-note">
            This returns a routing decision. It does not start a GitHub coding agent, approve a
            change, or deploy software.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="architecture-section live-section" aria-labelledby="live-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">What "live" means in this demo</p>
                <h2 id="live-title">Operations events</h2>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
            The Agent Operations page loads recent event history and listens for new API events.
            An accepted event is saved before the API broadcasts it to connected dashboards.
        </p>
        <ol class="flow-track" aria-label="Live operations event flow">
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">1</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>API records event</h3>
                    <p>Operations or routing work produces an event.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">2</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Saved in SQL</h3>
                    <p>Bounded event history is persisted before broadcast.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">3</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>SignalR broadcast</h3>
                    <p>The API's SignalR hub sends the accepted event to listeners.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">4</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Operations page</h3>
                    <p>Connected dashboards add the new event to the recent list.</p>
                </div>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="flow-note">
            This page explains the configured path; it does not show current event counts or prove
            that a live cloud service is connected.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="architecture-section" aria-labelledby="boundaries-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Who can see or change what</p>
                <h2 id="boundaries-title">Data boundaries</h2>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="boundary-grid">
            <article class="boundary-card">
                <h3>Browser</h3>
                <p>Displays the booking and operations screens and calls the API over HTTPS.</p>
                <p><strong>No direct database access.</strong></p>
            </article>
            <article class="boundary-card">
                <h3>Booking API</h3>
                <p>Applies booking rules and uses its managed identity to access SQL and configured AI services.</p>
                <p><strong>Operations event writes require an Entra role outside Development.</strong></p>
            </article>
            <article class="boundary-card">
                <h3>Azure SQL</h3>
                <p>Holds hotel and room catalog data, reservations, and bounded operations-event history.</p>
                <p><strong>The API database role is restricted.</strong></p>
            </article>
            <article class="boundary-card">
                <h3>Revisioned knowledge</h3>
                <p>Azure AI Search contains indexed chunks of canonical repository knowledge used for routing evidence checks.</p>
                <p><strong>It is not the hotel catalog or reservation store.</strong></p>
            </article>
        </div>
        <p class="flow-note">
            Application Insights is configured for API telemetry, with Log Analytics as its
            workspace. Configuration is not proof that a particular event is currently being emitted.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="architecture-section delivery-section" aria-labelledby="delivery-title">
        <div class="section-heading">
            <span class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">How a change reaches the demo</p>
                <h2 id="delivery-title">Software delivery</h2>
            </div>
        </div>
        <p class="section-intro">
            This is a separate path from runtime routing. Boards tracks the requirement; GitHub
            stores code and runs the cloud development and verification workflows.
        </p>
        <ol class="flow-track" aria-label="Software delivery flow">
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">1</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Azure Boards</h3>
                    <p>Holds the agreed requirement and its work state.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">2</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>GitHub repository</h3>
                    <p>Holds source, versioned knowledge, branches, and pull requests.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">3</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>Cloud development &amp; checks</h3>
                    <p>Agents implement a bounded change; review and QA gather evidence.</p>
                </div>
                <span class="flow-link" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            </li>
            <li class="flow-node">
                <span class="step-number">4</span>
                <div class="node-card">
                    <h3>GitHub Actions</h3>
                    <p>Verified changes can deploy the development web app and API to Azure.</p>
                </div>
            </li>
        </ol>
        <p class="release-boundary">
            <strong>Production is separate:</strong> a release proposal still requires explicit
            human approval and a manual promotion. A successful development deployment does not
            deploy production.
        </p>
    </section>
</section>
